  6.2 Why reviewer authority matters

  Admission to a committee, like admission to membership, is a governance decision. If the person who applies can also approve, the separation between applicant and competent organ disappears. That undermines:
  - the legitimacy of committee composition, which feeds positions, permissions and the forum;
  - auditability: reviewed_by is never even stored.

  Scoping authority to the organisation prevents an outsider, or a member of another organisation, from shaping the governance bodies of an organisation they do not belong to. The ratified rule "Membership stores evidence; Governance owns authority" (R §8) applies in the same way to committee bodies. The rule itself is currently undefined, so it must be decided, not inferred.

